"82_vm_errors.mst — E4: the VM's own failures become signals
 (docs/exceptions_design.md E4).

 Until now `1/0`, a doesNotUnderstand and an out-of-range index all ended
 the computation outright: `Object>>error:` is primitive 95, which reports
 and terminates, and every failure in the world funnels through it. So the
 exception system E0–E3 built could catch anything the LIBRARY chose to
 signal and nothing the VM raised — `[ 1/0 ] on: ZeroDivide do: [...]` was
 the obvious thing to write and the one thing that did not work.

 **The compatibility hinge, and it is the whole design.** `error:` now
 signals; `Exception>>unhandled` calls `primError:`, which is the untouched
 primitive 95. So a world with no `on:do:` anywhere behaves BYTE-IDENTICALLY
 to before — same message, same PROBE dossier, same curtailment blocks, same
 fatal exit — because when nothing handles the signal, the very same
 primitive runs at the very same point. That is what keeps the differential
 gate meaningful across this slice, and it is why E4 could be purely
 additive.

 It also fixes the debugger interaction §3.4 predicted: halt-on-error hangs
 off primitive 95, so it now fires on UNHANDLED errors only. A handled
 `[ ... ] on: Error do: [...]` no longer parks the primary at the raise —
 which is the confusion that hung a scripted `evaluate` for a session
 (applescript_design.md).

 **Load order.** The specific exception classes live in world/78, which loads
 long after the early library files that raise. A raise site in world/06
 cannot NAME `ZeroDivide` — the loader resolves globals at compile time. So
 the sites send an ordinary message (`self signalZeroDivide:`) and the
 late-bound implementation lives here, where the classes are visible. That is
 the same constraint that put E2's verbs in a re-opened Exception block.

 **And a real bug this slice exposes.** `Array>>at:` was a bare
 `<primitive: 26>` with no fallback body. The primitive bounds-checks
 correctly and fails — but a failed primitive with no fallback answers the
 RECEIVER, so `#(1 2) at: 9` quietly answered `#(1 2)` rather than raising.
 Every out-of-range read returned a silent wrong answer. Giving those methods
 fallbacks that signal is part of E4 rather than a separate fix, because
 until E4 there was nothing for them to signal."

nil subclass: Object [
    "The raw primitive-95 path, under its own name. `unhandled` calls this,
     so the signalling `error:` below cannot recurse into itself — and this
     is also the exact point at which behaviour is unchanged from before E4."
    primError: aString <String> [
        <primitive: 95>
    ]

    "E4: raise a catchable Error. Everything in the world that fails funnels
     through here, so this one method makes `1/0`, `subclassResponsibility`,
     `key not found` and the rest catchable at a stroke — while the unhandled
     path stays primError:, byte-identical to before."
    error: aString <String> [
        ^Error new signal: aString
    ]

    "── the specific kinds, reachable from files that load BEFORE world/78 ──
     Each is an ordinary send at the raise site, so the early file never has
     to name a class it cannot see yet."

    signalZeroDivide: aString <String> ^ <Object> [
        ^ZeroDivide new signal: aString
    ]
    signalIndexOutOfBounds: anIndex <Object> ^ <Object> [
        ^(IndexOutOfBounds new index: anIndex)
            signal: 'index out of range: ' , anIndex printString
    ]
    signalKeyNotFound: aKey <Object> ^ <Object> [
        ^(KeyNotFound new key: aKey)
            signal: 'key not found: ' , aKey printString
    ]

    "doesNotUnderstand: is already a world-level hook — the VM sends it — so
     E4 needs no VM change here either. The exception carries the message and
     the receiver, which is the whole reason MessageNotUnderstood has those
     ivars (E0 defined them and nothing filled them in until now)."
    doesNotUnderstand: aMessage <Message> ^ <Object> [
        ^((MessageNotUnderstood new message: aMessage) receiver: self)
            signal: 'does not understand ' , aMessage selector asString
    ]
]

"── the silent out-of-bounds, fixed ──────────────────────────────────────
 A bare <primitive:> with no fallback answers the receiver when the
 primitive fails. These four had exactly that shape, so every out-of-range
 access was a silent wrong answer rather than an error. The fallback runs
 only on failure, so the fast path is untouched."

ArrayedCollection subclass: Array [
    at: i <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 26>
        ^self signalIndexOutOfBounds: i
    ]
    at: i <Integer> put: v <Object> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 27>
        ^self signalIndexOutOfBounds: i
    ]
]

ArrayedCollection subclass: String [
    "String>>at: reads through basicByteAt:, whose own failure answered the
     String — which then re-entered Character value: and ran the stack out.
     Bounds-checking here answers the honest error instead."
    at: i <Integer> ^ <Character> [
        (i isInteger and: [ i >= 1 and: [ i <= self size ] ])
            ifFalse: [ ^self signalIndexOutOfBounds: i ].
        ^Character value: (self basicByteAt: i)
    ]
    at: i <Integer> put: v <Character|Integer> ^ <Character|Integer> [
        (i isInteger and: [ i >= 1 and: [ i <= self size ] ])
            ifFalse: [ ^self signalIndexOutOfBounds: i ].
        self basicByteAt: i put: (v isCharacter ifTrue: [ v value ] ifFalse: [ v ]).
        ^v
    ]
]
